Reject BC and AD where DuckDB expects a time zone name.

Since 2.0 a cast to TIMESTAMP ignores a trailing time zone name instead of
failing, as PostgreSQL does. PostgreSQL reads BC and AD as the era, though,
while DuckDB took them for a zone, so '0001-01-01 00:00:00 BC' silently became
0001-01-01 AD. pg_lake loads such values from CSV and relies on them being
rejected or range-checked.

diff --git a/src/common/types/timestamp.cpp b/src/common/types/timestamp.cpp
index 9af372bf170..366e5636a3f 100644
--- a/src/common/types/timestamp.cpp
+++ b/src/common/types/timestamp.cpp
@@ -107,6 +107,15 @@ TimestampCastResult Timestamp::TryConvertTimestampTZ(const char *str, idx_t len,
 				continue;
 			}
 			auto tz_len = str + pos - tz_name;
+			// BC and AD are eras rather than time zones: a timestamp that is ignoring its time zone would otherwise
+			// read a BC value as the same AD one
+			if (tz_len == 2) {
+				auto first = StringUtil::CharacterToUpper(tz_name[0]);
+				auto second = StringUtil::CharacterToUpper(tz_name[1]);
+				if ((first == 'B' && second == 'C') || (first == 'A' && second == 'D')) {
+					return TimestampCastResult::ERROR_INCORRECT_FORMAT;
+				}
+			}
 			if (tz_len) {
 				tz = string_t(tz_name, UnsafeNumericCast<uint32_t>(tz_len));
 			}
